# Personally authored curriculum; tuples are (prompt, chapters/slides, teaching, remaining components).
# Exact source excerpts are attached by build.py. This file adds no external medical facts.
ROWS = [
(1,'40:8-18,20-22,32-36,48-55,57-62,66-73','The digestive tract moves material from mouth to anus while accessory organs supply secretions. Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis and outer covering provide distinct layers. Villi and microvilli enlarge the absorbing surface; the large intestine recovers water and electrolytes. Acid, bile, movement and antibodies help shape the microbial distribution. The liver processes absorbed nutrients; the gallbladder stores and concentrates bile; the exocrine pancreas supplies enzymes and alkaline fluid.','Complete flora functions and named-organism inventory are not supplied. Autonomic salivary direction and Meissner plexus location need instructor clarification before testing.'),
(2,'41:23-38,40-58,60-66,68-75,76-98,100-116,117-129;42:6-15,17-32,34-41,51-59,61-72','Compare disorders by the disrupted function: an obstructed lumen prevents flow; an incompetent sphincter permits reflux; pancreatic enzyme loss impairs digestion; villous injury impairs absorption. Inflammatory bowel lesions differ in continuity and depth. Accessory-organ disease can change bile flow, hepatocyte processing or pancreatic enzyme delivery. The chapter cards below teach the supplied manifestations and broad treatments.','The guide requests pathophysiology, etiology, prevention, manifestations, diagnostics, treatment and complications for all GI/accessory disorders. Slides do not supply a complete disorder-by-component matrix or clinical protocols. See the detailed chapter topic ledger; absent prevention and diagnostic sequences remain gaps.'),
(3,'40:25-30','Mucus is a protective barrier at the gastric surface. Prostaglandins and nitric oxide protect this mucosal barrier. Protection must be distinguished from acid secretion: hydrochloric acid helps process food but is not the protective mucus layer.',''),
(4,'40:10,13-18,20-30,34-47,62-63,72-73','Digestion combines mechanical mixing with chemical breakdown. Salivary and pancreatic amylases process starch; pancreatic proteases act on proteins; emulsification separates fat droplets before lipolysis. Sugars and amino acids enter villus capillaries; absorbed lipid products leave through lacteals. Segmentation mixes contents against the surface, whereas peristalsis propels them.','Full enzyme activation cascade is not consistently worded on slide73; exclude that wording pending clarification.'),
(5,'41:32;42:12-16','Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis narrows the outlet through enlarged pyloric muscle. The packet distinguishes increased cell size from increased cell number; both contribute in the notes to slide16. Family history, maternal gastrin and listed maternal factors are risk associations. Adult outlet obstruction can be acquired or congenital.','The infant cause is described as unknown in slide16 notes. Risk associations are not proof of a single cause. Specific acquired adult causes are not enumerated.'),
(6,'41:53-54','After partial gastrectomy, a bariatric procedure or pyloroplasty, loss of reservoir and emptying control allows hypertonic chyme to enter the intestine rapidly. Early symptoms include cramps, diarrhea, fullness and nausea. Late symptoms at one to three hours include weakness, sweating and confusion. The supplied dietary strategy uses small frequent meals, more protein and fewer carbohydrates, and fluids between meals.','Formal diagnostic criteria, full osmotic/hormonal mechanism, prevention before surgery and detailed complication management are absent.'),
(7,'40:14,19','Saliva contains water, mucus, electrolytes, salivary alpha-amylase and IgA. Amylase begins carbohydrate digestion; IgA is the listed immunoglobulin.','The tooth-decay prevention mechanism is not supplied. Salivary autonomic statements are inconsistent and not graded.'),
(8,'41:93,103-107;42:65-68','HAV and HEV are described with fecal-oral spread; HBV with parenteral and sexual transmission; HCV with blood exposure and chronic liver disease. HDV requires HBV for replication. Adult slides divide the symptomatic course into prodromal, icteric and recovery phases. Autoimmune hepatitis is a separate immune-mediated category.','Type-specific diagnostic serologies, full pathophysiology, treatment regimens and complication profiles are incomplete. Ch42 slide68 blanket no-curative-therapy statement conflicts with antiviral treatment wording and is excluded. Maternal timing restrictions and dated statistics need clarification.'),
(9,'41:16-19;42:55-59','Osmotic diarrhea follows a nonabsorbed substance drawing water into the lumen. Secretory diarrhea reflects secretion of chloride/bicarbonate-rich fluid or reduced net sodium absorption. Excessive motility reduces transit time and the opportunity for absorption. Lactase deficiency illustrates the osmotic route: unabsorbed lactose contributes to diarrhea and gas.','Inflammatory small-volume classification in released Ch41 Q1 conflicts with slide62 wording; that volume distinction is not scored.'),
(10,'42:55-57;41:18','Children have lower fluid reserves than adults, so prolonged diarrhea can rapidly threaten hydration. The packet emphasizes replacing fluids and electrolytes and maintaining nutrition. General diarrhea consequences include electrolyte imbalance, metabolic acidosis and weight loss.','Age-specific severity thresholds, shock assessment and replacement calculations are not supplied.'),
(11,'42:25,27,34-35','Meconium is intestinal material present before birth; abnormally sticky meconium can obstruct the intestine. Distal intestinal obstruction syndrome is explicitly associated with cystic fibrosis and thick intestinal contents, especially with dehydration and inadequate pancreatic enzymes.','The neonatal meconium-ileus/CF association is not explicitly established in these slide passages; do not equate the older distal-obstruction label with every neonatal case. Obtain the textbook passage.'),
(12,'41:32-38;42:17-27,31-32','Distinguish a lesion blocking the bowel from functional failure of motility. Adhesions are a common small-bowel cause; large-bowel causes include cancer, volvulus and strictures. Obstruction level changes the pattern of distention and vomiting. In children, congenital narrowing, malrotation, Hirschsprung disease and intussusception provide different mechanisms. Telescoping bowel can compress its mesentery, obstruct venous return and progress to ischemic injury.','A complete prevention/diagnostic algorithm and complication inventory by obstruction type are not provided.'),
(13,'40:64-65;41:88-89','Bilirubin comes from breakdown of aged red blood cells. Unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble; hepatocytes conjugate it with glucuronic acid, making it water soluble (slide65 notes). Intestinal bacteria convert downstream bilirubin products to urobilinogen. Jaundice can reflect excessive production, hepatic processing disease or obstruction of bile flow.','Full transport, recycling and excretion fractions are absent. Slide64 pigment simplification needs textbook clarification before detailed cycle testing.'),
(14,'40:58-60,65-66','Kupffer cells are phagocytic macrophages in the hepatic sinusoids. The notes to slide65 explicitly connect them with removal of bacteria and foreign particles from hepatic blood. Distinguish their immune role from hepatocyte secretion and stellate-cell participation in fibrosis.',''),
(15,'41:76-87,94-95;42:70-73','Resistance to portal blood flow raises pressure. Collateral varices can appear in the esophagus, stomach, rectum and abdominal wall; rupture can bleed severely. Splenic enlargement can accompany thrombocytopenia. Ascites adds fluid to the peritoneal cavity. Pediatric extrahepatic disease commonly reflects portal-vein obstruction; intrahepatic disease is associated with cirrhosis or congenital hepatic fibrosis.','Exact cause-by-location inventory and pediatric management details are incomplete; slide72 merely refers back to adult treatment.'),
(16,'41:26-27;42:28-30','Adult GERD allows acid and pepsin to reflux through a weak or transiently relaxed lower esophageal sphincter. Increased abdominal pressure can contribute. Symptoms extend beyond heartburn to cough and laryngeal complaints. Infant reflux can reflect immature neuromuscular control and often resolves; persistent injury, bleeding, dysphagia or poor growth changes the concern.','Complete prevention, diagnostic testing and age-specific medication protocols are not supplied. Feeding/position changes are only course overview, not a safe-sleep protocol.'),
(17,'40:25-30,44,69;41:58-59;42:39-41','Intrinsic factor supports ileal vitamin B12 absorption; gastroferrin is linked to intestinal iron absorption in the packet. Bile supports absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. A, D, E and K are identified as fat soluble; listed deficiency consequences include night blindness, bone effects, neurologic effects and bleeding tendency.','Complete water-soluble classification, iron absorption/recycling/storage cycle and comprehensive deficiency comparisons are absent. No invented iron cycle is tested.'),
(18,'40:34-44','Villi and microvilli increase small-intestinal surface area. Major nutrient digestion and absorption occur in the small intestine. Sugars are primarily absorbed in the duodenum and upper jejunum. Water recovery occurs largely in the small intestine; products of carbohydrate/protein digestion enter capillaries, while lipid transport uses lacteals.','Full nutrient-by-segment map is not supplied.'),
(19,'41:94-102;42:69-72','Inflammation and fibrosis reorganize the liver and reduce function. Distortion and obstruction can raise portal pressure and shunt blood away from liver tissue. Alcohol-associated disease spans steatosis, hepatitis and fibrosis; biliary disease has different duct-related mechanisms. Pediatric cirrhosis can also impair growth, nutrition and development.','Complete prevention, diagnostic criteria, treatment and complication management for each adult/pediatric cause are absent.'),
(20,'42:34-36,25;41:56','In cystic fibrosis, very thick mucus obstructs pancreatic ducts and limits digestive enzyme delivery. Pancreatic insufficiency produces maldigestion, especially of fat; growth failure can follow. The packet lists pancreatic enzyme replacement, high-calorie/high-protein intake and supplements. Pancreatic damage can involve beta cells and glucose regulation.',''),
(21,'42:37-41','Gluten-sensitive enteropathy causes T-cell-mediated epithelial injury, inflammation and villous flattening. Reduced absorbing surface produces greasy bulky stools, poor growth and nutritional deficiencies. The packet lists serology and duodenal biopsy, then a permanent gluten-free diet and supplementation. Damaged villi can also produce temporary lactose intolerance.','Prevention before disease onset is not supplied. Oats/malt blanket grain classification and meconium-ileus wording in slides38–39 are excluded pending clarification; avoid adding dietary details from memory.'),
(22,'41:10-12,35-36','The vomiting center is in the medulla. Retching is the action without expulsion; projectile vomiting need not be preceded by nausea or retching. Repeated vomiting can disturb fluid, electrolytes and acid-base balance. The obstruction-level examples distinguish clear gastric fluid, bile-stained fluid and later feculent material.','Specific chemoreceptor/trigger-zone pathways and reliable medication-by-cause selection are incomplete. Slide11 groups potentially inconsistent agents; no drug-selection key is based on that list.'),
(23,'40:23-30,36-43,52,62-63,71-73','Separate the source cell from its product and effect. Parietal cells provide acid, intrinsic factor and gastroferrin; chief cells provide pepsinogen; G cells gastrin; ECL cells histamine; D cells somatostatin. CCK links fat processing to slower emptying and gallbladder contraction. Pancreatic enzymes act on nutrient classes, whereas bile salts emulsify fat.','The requested complete enzyme/hormone/cell/deficiency/excess inventory exceeds the packet. Pancreatic activation wording on slide73 and cholesterol-ester product on slide42 require clarification.'),
(24,'43:8-28','Bone combines collagen-based tensile strength with mineral rigidity. Osteoblasts form osteoid, osteocytes coordinate maintenance and osteoclasts resorb bone. RANKL promotes osteoclast activity; OPG acts as a decoy. Remodeling alternates activation, resorption and new formation. Fracture repair proceeds through hematoma, procallus, callus, replacement and remodeling.',''),
(25,'45:6-9;43:25','Primary ossification centers develop in the diaphysis and secondary centers in the epiphyses. Growth continues at the physeal plate through endochondral ossification until closure unites epiphysis and metaphysis. Nutrition, general health, growth hormone and growth regulators influence the process.',''),
(26,'43:52,64','The supplied slides describe creatine kinase as a marker increased with diseased or damaged muscle and urinary myoglobin with acute muscle damage. These are injury statements.','No supplied passage explicitly establishes the lab value associated with muscle mass. Slide52 also confuses creatinine/creatine kinase terminology. The requested mass association is Missing and has no scored item.'),
(27,'45:47-51,61','DMD affects dystrophin, which anchors the actin cytoskeleton to the basement membrane. Poor anchorage allows contraction-related membrane injury, calcium entry and fiber necrosis. Progressive weakness, Gower sign, respiratory insufficiency and cardiomyopathy are listed. Enlarged calves can reflect fat/connective tissue rather than effective muscle (slide61 notes).','Formal diagnostic testing pathway is absent. Absolute males-only language and conflicting calf explanation are not used as scored claims.'),
(28,'45:22-23','Osteogenesis imperfecta involves defective collagen production in bone and/or vessels. Frequent fractures and bony deformation can accompany blue sclera, poor dentition and vascular weakness. Careful handling and prompt fracture care are listed management principles.',''),
(29,'44:49-52','Osteoarthritis features local articular-cartilage loss, subchondral changes and osteophytes. Pain, stiffness, limited movement and finger-node enlargement can occur. Contrast this structural pattern with systemic autoimmune synovial disease. Exercise, weight management when relevant and joint support appear in the supplied treatment overview.','Specific etiologic pathway and primary prevention evidence are not complete; do not present treatment measures as proven prevention. Avoid a simplistic inflammatory/noninflammatory binary.'),
(30,'44:54-58,52','Rheumatoid arthritis is systemic autoimmune injury involving synovium and joints. Symmetric swelling, deformity, tenderness and systemic inflammation support the distinction from local degenerative change. The slide52 notes describe pannus as thick abnormal synovial granulation tissue after T-cell/TNF interactions.','Etiology beyond autoimmunity and the full causal sequence are not supplied.'),
(31,'44:27-28;43:64','Rapid muscle breakdown releases intracellular contents, including myoglobin. The supplied classic triad is muscle pain, weakness and dark urine. Management emphasizes prompt intravenous hydration to protect kidney flow; hyperkalemia may require temporary dialysis.',''),
(32,'44:62-66','Gout follows disrupted uric-acid production or excretion. Sufficient concentration permits crystallization and deposition; persistent deposits form tophi. Recurrent monoarticular inflammation, often at the great toe, may accompany renal disease and stones.',''),
(33,'44:30-35;43:61-62','Osteoporosis reduces bone mass and structural integrity through imbalance between resorption and formation. Hormone loss, inactivity and glucocorticoid effects on RANKL/OPG are supplied contributors. Fractures, height loss and kyphosis are manifestations; weightbearing activity and sufficient mineral intake are prevention measures in the packet.','Exact x-ray appearance and complete diagnostic criteria are absent. Slide30 poorly-mineralized wording and absolute density cutoffs are not used to distinguish osteoporosis from osteomalacia.'),
(34,'44:38','Paget disease involves excessive bone resorption and formation. Accelerated remodeling enlarges and softens affected bone; the axial skeleton is commonly involved. Do not equate high turnover with stronger architecture.',''),
(35,'44:59-61','Ankylosing spondylitis is chronic inflammatory disease of spinal or sacroiliac joints with stiffening and fusion. HLA-B27 is a strong association, while the cause is unknown. Restricted back motion and reduced chest movement are supplied manifestations.',''),
(36,'45:26-28','Structural scoliosis includes vertebral rotation; nonstructural curvature arises outside the spine, such as posture or unequal leg length (slide28 notes). Bracing aims to prevent progression, and fusion is a supplied surgical option.','Pulmonary-function threshold, cardiopulmonary complications and full treatment-selection criteria are absent. No numeric curvature threshold is invented.'),
(37,'45:45-46','Cerebral palsy is a nonprogressive movement/posture disorder caused by injury or malformation of the developing CNS. Failure to reach motor milestones is a supplied finding.','Specific prenatal, perinatal and postnatal etiologies are not enumerated.'),
(38,'45:55-57;44:44-45','Osteosarcoma arises from bone-producing mesenchymal cells and makes osteoid; long-bone metaphyses, often around the knee, are emphasized in Chapter44. Ewing sarcoma is a malignant round-cell tumor involving diaphyses or flat bones. Rhabdomyosarcoma arises from muscle precursors and is a soft-tissue comparison, not a bone-producing tumor.',''),
(39,'45:15-18','DDH involves the proximal femur, acetabulum or both. Risk factors include family history, breech presentation and oligohydramnios. Limited abduction, unequal folds and limb-length discrepancy support evaluation. Ortolani denotes a dislocated but reducible hip; Barlow a reduced but dislocatable hip. Earlier intervention has better supplied outcomes.','Primary prevention, imaging pathway and complete complication profile are incomplete.'),
(40,'45:39-40','Legg-Calve-Perthes disease interrupts femoral-head blood supply. The ossification center can become necrotic, collapse and later remodel. Knee pain can accompany restricted hip motion and abnormal gait. Containment and preserving movement are supplied management goals.','Underlying cause of the vascular interruption is not supplied.'),
(41,'45:22,34-36;44:81-83','The packet covers collagen-related osteogenesis imperfecta and juvenile arthritis, with general autoimmune muscle/connective-tissue inflammation in Chapter44. These do not establish an exhaustive pediatric connective-tissue disease list.','The intended pediatric connective-tissue disease inventory and distinguishing mechanisms/features need the matching textbook passage or instructor clarification.'),
(42,'43:30-36;44:8-18','Classify a joint by movement separately from its connecting tissue. Synarthrosis, amphiarthrosis and diarthrosis describe immobile, slightly mobile and freely mobile joints; fibrous, cartilaginous and synovial describe structure. Classify a fracture by completeness, skin opening, orientation and mechanism. A torus injury buckles the cortex; a greenstick injury disrupts one cortex; comminution creates multiple fragments.','Individual named fibrous-joint definitions rely on a conflicted released key; its correction remains explicit. Full subtype geometry needs textbook figures where absent.'),
(43,'43:33-37,44;44:20,23','Articular cartilage pads bony surfaces; synovial membrane lines the capsule; synovial fluid lubricates and nourishes. Tendons join muscle to bone and ligaments join bone to bone. Bursae are synovial-lined, fluid-filled sacs among tendons, muscles and bony prominences.',''),
(44,'43:36','Articular cartilage lacks vessels and nerves and is insensitive to pain. This does not mean that the whole joint is painless: its capsule is richly innervated.',''),
(45,'43:50-56','Excitation carries the action potential into the muscle; t-tubule signaling opens calcium-release channels. Coupling makes actin available to bind myosin. Cross-bridge action shortens the sarcomere; relaxation follows detachment and calcium return to the sarcoplasmic reticulum.','Complete troponin/tropomyosin and ATP-binding/hydrolysis sequence is not supplied.'),
(46,'43:57-59','In isometric contraction, tension increases while muscle length stays constant and the limb does not move. Dynamic contraction changes length: concentric shortens and eccentric lengthens. The agonist contracts as prime mover while the antagonist relaxes.',''),
(47,'43:45-48,53-57','The CNS controls voluntary skeletal muscle through motor units. A unit consists of an anterior horn cell, its axon and supplied muscle fibers. Recruitment and discharge frequency grade force; spindles signal stretching. Calcium-release channels link the electrical signal to contractile availability.','Complete neurotransmitter/receptor sequence at the neuromuscular junction is absent.'),
(48,'43:17,21','Calcium and phosphate form the inorganic mineral component of bone. Mineralization follows matrix production; hydroxyapatite is the named end product.',''),
(49,'45:12-13;43:42','The packet contrasts muscle as about 25% of infant body weight with 40% in adults. Muscle distribution changes with growth; fibers enlarge as fibrils become more numerous. Separately, the muscle tissue composition is about 75% water and 20% protein. Do not confuse tissue composition with percentage of body weight.',''),
(50,'45:37-43','Osgood-Schlatter disease involves the tibial tubercle and anterior patellar tendon. Activity-related pain, swelling and pressure tenderness guide the comparison. The supplied treatment reduces strenuous activity; persistent pain may require a cast or brace.','Specific prevention, diagnostic testing and long-term complications are not supplied. Broad osteochondrosis vascular wording needs instructor clarification before a precise causal claim is scored.'),
(51,'44:39-40;45:29-33','Osteomyelitis is bone infection that can spread beneath periosteum and into marrow. Loss of blood supply creates dead bone (sequestrum), while new periosteal bone is involucrum. Infants can stop moving a limb; older children can develop fever, swelling and reduced weightbearing.',''),
(52,'45:34-36','Juvenile idiopathic arthritis shares inflammatory joint injury with adult RA but differs in presentation. The packet highlights larger joints, less severe pain, cervical changes and chronic uveitis; rheumatoid factor is seldom detected.','Full etiology and subtype criteria are incomplete; slide34 leaves exactly five joints undefined. Blanket antibody/nodule statements need clarification and are excluded from scored keys.')]
